WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.390
Film Courage: What are things you see most
beginning writers get wrong with writing an

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:05.390
outline?

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:06.850
Alan Watt, Author, Screenwriter and Founder
of L.A. Writers’ Lab: Plot.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:12.400
So many writers think that writing an outline
is figuring out their plot.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.220
There’s something very satisfying about
coming up with the plot.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.839
They are like Oh here…I’ve got it!But
here is the problem, again Einstein says you

00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:25.880
can’t solve a problem at the same level
of consciousness that created the problem.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.009
Every story begins with a dramatic problem.

00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:31.550
And what happens is (ever writer has had this
experience) you get really excited by your

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:36.990
premise and you start writing it and you get
halfway through and then you get stuck and

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:42.570
then you are trying to make these characters
interesting but you feel like they are pieces

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.420
on a chest board that you are trying to move
around.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.510
And so it’s important to understand that
story structure is not about plotting.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.130
Story structure is about the DNA of your protagonist’s
transformation.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:01.079
So what you really want to do is you want
to start to be curious about your protagonist’s

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:07.729
dilemma because when you really understand
the nature of their struggle, nature of their

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:14.920
dilemma, it becomes the source of your story
and it starts to pay off in silver dollars.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:25.009
It starts to give you all sorts of ideas,
images and scenarios that are going to become

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:27.780
your plot.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.469
So here is what I would suggest, if you want
to come up with a great outline, the first

00:01:32.469 --> 00:01:38.799
thing we do in the 90-day novel and the 90-day
screenplay is we spend one week imagining

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:44.500
the world of the story and doing absolutely
no outlining, because what happens is I would

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.450
say our idea of the story is never the whole
story, if you start to outline your idea of

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:55.609
your story you are going to get stuck, it’s
going to become kind of a superficial thing.

00:01:55.609 --> 00:02:02.319
But if you allow yourself to lose yourself
in your characters relating to other characters,

00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:03.469
they are going to surprise you.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:04.469
They are going to be alive.

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:09.280
They are going to start doing things that
you might not have been imagined had you forced

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.110
them into your prescribed outline.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.280
So that’s the first step is imagine the
world of your story, allow your characters

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:24.530
to be relating to each other in ways without
imposing any structure in the outline whatsoever.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.510
Now that you’ve got all of this raw material
you start to see how they are relating.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:34.990
Let me give you an example, you would never
if you were writing IT’S A WONDERFUL LIFE,

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:35.990
what does he want?

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.920
He wants to leave Bedford Falls in order to
have a wonderful life.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:44.070
The mid-point of that is where Mr. Potter
offers Jimmy Stewart a job.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.440
That would never…it you are outlining your
story that has nothing to do with what Jimmy

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.980
Stewart wants.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.190
Mr. Potter is the devil, he’s the enemy.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.220
Why would he ever offer him a job, that wouldn’t
make any sense.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.130
But if you are imagining the world of the
story you come up with the mid-point structure

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:10.000
questions which is how is your protagonist
experience temptation.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.670
It might occur to you that the devil would
tempt him with a big job offer.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:22.000
That’s where I feel like how I teach story
structure is different.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.930
I’ve been told it’s different than everybody
and it’s because there is no plotting at

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:29.930
all.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.270
There are these structure questions, they
are experiential questions.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:44.150
We are exploring your protagonist’s experience
at key stages in the journey.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.710
I hesitate to say “the hero’s journey”
because then people get the fixed idea “Oh!

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:49.740
You are teaching The Hero’s Journey.”

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.860
No, I’m actually not.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:57.840
I’m teaching is that story structure is
an immutable paradigm for a spiritual transformation

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.490
and that there are key stages that one goes
through including the reluctance at the end

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.600
of the first act, the false hope (which we
just talked about) and now we’re talking

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.350
about the mid-point, temptation.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:13.731
And then suffering toward the end of the second
act and then finally surrender at the end

00:04:13.731 --> 00:04:16.530
of the second act.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:24.770
These are all experiences, reluctance, false
hope, temptation, suffering, surrender.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.110
They have nothing to do with plotting (and
we’re not talking about reversal).

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:34.389
Film Courage: As someone already figuring
out the ending in that before they are figuring

00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:37.940
this outline you said think about it for a
week.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.820
Think about the crux of the story, what is
it really about.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:46.270
Are they also figuring out the ending or is
it revealed sometimes after you do the outline?

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.440
Al: Both…it can get revealed after you do
the outline.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.220
I don’t like the word figuring out because
I don’t think we figure anything out.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:05:01.240
But I have an exercise that I give my students
to experience the end and it’s a two-part

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.240
exercise.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.250
I say imagine your protagonist transformed
at the end of the story.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:10.590
How is your protagonist relating differently
to other characters at the end of the story?

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:16.070
How are they relating differently than they
were at the beginning and what do they understand

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:19.070
that they didn’t understand at the beginning?

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:24.410
Do that everyday, do that for 10 minutes every
day.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:27.980
And you start to plant a flag for your protagonist
at the end of the story.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:32.510
You start to experience your protagonist at
the end of the story.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.320
Character suggests plot.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:39.770
When you start to experience your character
relating differently to these other characters,

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:40.830
plot naturally emerges.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.830
But you are not figuring anything out.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.170
If you try to figure it out you are going
to get stuck.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:52.400
And then the second thing you want to do is
you want to imagine the climax of your story

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.510
remember that we want our protagonist to be
active through the whole story.

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:59.550
You want to have an active protagonist.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:03.160
And the challenge for writers is that we tend
to be sort of passive observers.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.050
We are always watching what is going on.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.130
And so when we sort of put ourselves into
the protagonist’s situation, sometimes the

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.889
protagonist can be the least interesting character
in the.

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:16.830
So you want to find a way to make them active.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:20.960
The structure questions are always going to
make your protagonist active.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:27.430
At the climax you want to think about the
difficult choice your protagonist makes between

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.949
what he or she wants and what they need.

00:06:29.949 --> 00:06:33.240
It’s a different choice so that’s an action.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:39.030
I’m making a choice so your protagonist
has to take an action between what they want

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:40.260
and what they need.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:47.090
So think in terms of let’s say I want justice
but I believe that when I get revenge justice

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:48.220
will be done.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:55.640
So what I want is actually revenge, I want
them to pay.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:00.550
And what I need is to have compassion, okay.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:04.650
So in other words the end of the story (the
climax of the story) is we are going to reframe

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:10.070
out relationship with the theme (i.e., justice)
and so I’m going to put my protagonist in

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:15.670
a situation where he can make a difficult
choice between what he wants (revenge) and

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:19.490
what he needs (is compassion).

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:24.290
In other words if it’s not a difficult choice
he would have made the choice in act one but

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:31.400
it has to be difficult and it doesn’t have
to be (sometimes we call it the battle scene

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:41.830
- the climax of the story) but it doesn’t
have to be an external battle, it can

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:43.240
be an internal battle that gets dramatized.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:50.699
If you think about Holly Hunter in BROADCAST
NEWS, the battle scene is her staring at a

00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:58.800
plane ticket on a bench at the airport because
William Hurt says “Here’s the ticket.”

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:05.009
What she wants is for there to be integrity
in broadcast journalism.

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:09.770
But what she needs is to have integrity so
when William Hurt crosses the line and fakes

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.960
a tear in this interview she’s furious.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.389
And she wants him to take responsibility for
it so that she can go off to this exotic island

00:08:18.389 --> 00:08:21.300
for a week and frolic with him.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.830
But he’s not going to.

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:29.180
And so she is forced to make a difficult choice
within herself.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:34.860
Am I going to get on the plane with William
Hurt or am I going to get back in the cab.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.510
And so that’s the battle scene but it’s
still active.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:42.860
She makes the choice of getting back into
the cab.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:48.820
So you want to find a way for your protagonist
to make a difficult choice between what they

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:53.240
want which is always outside of themselves
(i.e., William Hurt) and what they need which

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.510
is always within (I’m going to do the right
thing).

